# Profy Changes Log

## 2026-10-07 (fix/codex-review-hardening, Codex review pass 19)
- [P2] an input "\x01" counted as an earlier match: masked text was
  replaced by "\x01", and "is this position masked" compared characters, so
  the compound rule leaned on any "\x01" in the input. "\x01hellenic" was
  masked "\x01****enic", "\x01cockatoo" "\x01****atoo" and "shell\x01hello"
  "s****\x01hello" (main leaves all three unchanged). Readings containing an
  input "\x01" were also rejected (block=["a\x01"] left "xa\x01" unmasked,
  where "a$" masks "xa$").
- Masked positions are now kept outside the text: `_detect` keeps a
  bytearray of masked positions, and the three readers use it: `masked()`,
  the "masked characters are never reused" check, and compaction in
  `_CandidateFinder`, which collapses runs of masked positions.
- The working text still shows masks as a placeholder, so no expression
  reads through one. Each dictionary picks it (`_placeholder`): the first
  candidate in every stop class of its expressions. The candidates are
  "\x01", then the private-use characters; none is a letter, digit, "_",
  "-" or whitespace, and none has case variants (asserted for every
  candidate).
- The placeholder is "\x01" unless a block entry makes that readable;
  block=["\x01x"] gives "". A block list that reads every candidate
  raises ValueError at construction (tested with a two-character candidate
  list).
- An input occurrence of the placeholder needs no neutralizing. No
  expression reads it, so it reads like a mask while the flags say it is
  not one, and the output is built from the original text as before.
- Audit of every in-band marker: "\x01" was the only one (10 occurrences:
  `masked()`, the reuse check, compaction and `_mask_spans`, plus six in
  comments and docstrings). Stop classes are built from the characters
  each expression reads; windowing uses stop classes and the changed spans;
  run shortening only removes characters; normalization keeps span maps; the
  pattern driver never masks working text.
- Tests:
  - Property test, both drivers, block lists (), ("zork",) and
    ("\x01x", "zork"): for "\x00"-"\x1f", "\x7f", U+FFFD, U+FFFF,
    U+E000, U+F8FF, U+F0000 and U+10FFFD next to clean words and
    profanities, clean words stay clean, the characters are kept exactly,
    and profanities are masked exactly. r19 fails 48 cases, all "\x01" with
    the regex driver.
  - Placeholder choice and exhaustion; the candidate properties.
  - The strategy-equivalence test gained three texts with such characters
    and a block list that moves the placeholder (4 cases, was 2).
- Probes against r19:
  - An input "\x01" reads exactly like "\x02" (masks, matches with
    severities, score), in about 3,130 texts per language × 4 languages ×
    both drivers × with and without block entries ($S/inv19.py). 0
    violations; r19 has 1,410.
  - Windowed or compacted vs full rescan on corpora joined by those
    characters, with and without the placeholder moved
    ($S/equiv19.py, 4 languages × 3 strategies × 2 block lists): 0 diffs.
  - Texts joined by those characters ($S/diff19.py, 6,000 per language,
    both drivers, with and without block entries): every difference
    contains "\x01", all with the regex driver.
- Differential vs r19 (no "\x01" in them): 40 corpus runs 0 differing
  results, 0 timeouts; sweep identical in all 4 languages; dictionary scan
  845 flagged, identical.
- Performance vs r19 (CPU time, best of 3): tiny checks, prose, clean and
  German throughput, and block filters with 50 and 200 words (build, prose,
  tiny) at parity, within run-to-run noise.
- core.py 1290 -> 1303 statements, 502 -> 508 branches.

## 2026-10-07 (fix/codex-review-hardening, Codex review pass 18)
- [P2] the block-entry precheck skipped valid matches: block=["zip"] left
  "zİp" and "z-İ-p" unmasked (main "***", "*****"). Its letter sets came from
  str.lower/upper/casefold, which never pair "i" with "İ" although
  re.IGNORECASE does. Each letter that is not optional now gets one pattern
  (`_first_character`) built from the same pieces as the expression (its
  class, the first character of each longer option, its literal) with the
  same flags, and `_may_match` searches the text's distinct characters with
  it, so the precheck decides exactly as the expression would. Property
  test: for 10 entries ("zip", "kiss", "straße", "ıdiot", "İzmir", "ǆep",
  "σoφoς", ...) every code point below 0x3000 that re.IGNORECASE matches
  with one of the entry's characters, substituted for it, is never skipped.
- Found by that test (main and every earlier round too): block and allow
  entries with a character whose lowercase is longer never matched their own
  text with either driver. "İzmir".lower() is "i̇zmir" (with a combining
  dot), so block=["İzmir"] left "İzmir" and "İZMIR" unmasked. Entries,
  occurrences and literal patterns are now folded by `_fold`, which keeps
  such characters (no bundled entry contains one; checked).
- [P3, confirmed] test_split_readings_stay_linear_without_run_shortening
  [block-only] patched `blocks.runs`, which detection never reads since
  pass 17 (each entry has its own shortener in `blocks.entries`). It now
  replaces the bundled shortener and every entry's shortener, and asserts
  each replacement was called. Audit of every test that patches internals:
  - added expressions are wrapped in a counting proxy, and the 9 tests
    using them assert each one matched;
  - the spacing test records the strategy each finder chose (forced
    spacings use only their own, the default spacing a non-full one);
  - the windowed test asserts the windowed mode;
  - the no-op resync test asserts the clock is never read.
  Patches whose assertions already exercised them: severity_map,
  false_positives, and the sync scripts' ROOT/which/run/fetch/urlopen.
- Timing tests for CI (shared runners, 3.9-3.14):
  - All of them measure CPU time (time.process_time), including the
    adversarial worker, the very-long-input test and the cached-helper test.
  - Scaling tests share `assert_linear`: the minimum of 3 alternating
    small/large repetitions, a ratio limit of ×8 for a 4× size step, and a
    20 s hang cap.
  - Sizes were raised so that linear is about ×4 and the old quadratic
    ×12 or more: cross-word 16000, hex-block 256000, distinct-hex 24000,
    glued copies 1280. The dense one-word test uses an 8× step with a ×16
    limit, because at 4× the pass-3 bug only reached ×7.3.
  - The adversarial test runs one filter per subprocess, sequentially, with
    a 10 s CPU budget per input and a 1800 s hard timeout.
  - Each test fails against the snapshot with the bug it guards, at a 4×
    step against ×8:
    - shapes (ca5cfaa8): ×13.5-13.6
    - cross-word (r6): ×12.0-12.1
    - hex-block (r6): ×12.1-13.8
    - distinct-hex (r7): ×12.5
    - dense one-word (c2004355): ×21.4 at the 8× step, limit ×16
    - glued (r12): ×13.3-15.2
    - block-only letter/both-ends (r14): ×14.5/×15.1
    - split block-only (r14): ×14.9
    - German s/ß/S/sS (r16): ×15.5-16.4
    - block-bundled (r16): ×14.3
    - adversarial (main): 28.9 s for "*" * 22, over the budget.
  - On r18 every test is at ×4.6 or below. Shapes with no buggy snapshot
