## 2026-10-07 (fix/codex-review-hardening, Codex review pass 17)
- [P2] one block entry unmasked another's reading ("x-y-z": block=["yz"]
  "x-***", with "xy" "***-z"): the block pipeline selected among all
  entries' readings and its masks blocked the rest in later passes. Each
  entry is now detected on its own (`_Dictionary.entries`: its expressions
  and its own run shortener; the normalized text computed once), and every
  reading that holds is masked (`_supported`: the largest set in which each
  reading needing a neighbour has one, by support counting, like the old
  chain resolution; overlapping readings all stay). Other entries only
  count as compound parts and remove false positives, which never makes a
  guard stricter; the bleed guard's "another entry spells it" rule and the
  prefix entries consider the entry alone (`_unbled(alone=True)`):
  "stupides" lost "stupide-s" once "stupide" was blocked too.
- [P2] pattern driver: block entries keep every occurrence
  (`_literal(deduplicate=False)`); the bundled ones keep upstream's
  deduplication.
- [P2] severity/score: every reported match takes the highest severity of
  its region (findings connected by overlaps; more findings only join
  regions), and `_score` counts the distinct spans found with their region's
  severity (more findings only add spans or raise them). "n!gger" with
  block=["n!gger"]: extreme, score 100 (was high, 60).
- Properties (both drivers, every minimum_severity, base lists of 0-4
  words plus one): masking, each masked character's severity and the score
  never go down. Probe ($S/blockmono17.py, 4 languages): r17 121 violations
  in 52,800 checks; final code 0 in 440,000. Suite: regression cases (the
  three findings, "stupides", "doofe") and a seeded property test that r17
  fails.
- Cost of detecting each entry on its own: an exact prefilter skips an
  entry when one of its non-optional letters (with case variants, "ſ" for
  "s") is absent from the text, and run indexes are built on first lookup.
  With 200 block words (dictionary words): prose 1.30s -> 1.42s (+9%), with
  50: +1%, tiny checks within 15%; building a filter with 200 new block words
  7ms -> 116ms (once, cached). Without block entries: throughput, tiny checks
  and adversarial worst cases at parity; "shit " * 32000 with a one-word
  filter -3%; 64k "12" block hits in one token 0.20s -> 0.21s.
  `Severity.weight` no longer builds a dict per call.
- Differential vs r17, all 40 corpus runs, comparing masks, matches with
  their severities, and scores: 0 differing results. Windowed/compacted vs
  full rescan: 0 diffs with 21 block entries. Sweep and dictionary scan
  identical.
- Test changes: `_score` takes (severities, counts); `_supported` unit test.
- core.py 1223 -> 1285 statements, 480 -> 502 branches (`_supported`,
  per-entry block dictionaries, regions, prefilter).

## 2026-10-07 (fix/codex-review-hardening, Codex review pass 16)
- Restructure: block entries no longer interleave with bundled detection.
  `ProfanityFilter.dictionary` is the bundled dictionary built without the
  block list (shared with filters without one); `ProfanityFilter.blocks`
  holds only the block entries (cached by its own options; allow applies
  to both; None when no entry is left). check() runs `_detect` for the
  bundled words exactly as without a block list, then for the block
  entries `_exact` (their own text in the visible input, every entry's
  occurrences kept, also where entries overlap) and `_detect` with the
  block dictionary; the mask is everything found, and `_report` gives one
  deterministic rule: text order, longer first, among equal spans an
  entry's own text, then a block reading, then a bundled one; each reports
  the part of its span no earlier report holds (one holding nothing new is
  not reported). "Block never reduces masking" holds by construction.
  The block dictionary spells its entries as the language normalizes the
  text, keeps the runs they need (its own shortener), and takes the
  bundled words as compound parts (context only).
- Deleted: the block-hit candidates and held/spare weighting
  (`_Candidate.spare`, `_outside`, `_sizes`), the hit/reading
  reconciliation (`_holds`, groups), the separate selection of block-only
  words (`_Dictionary.added`), `_block_hits` (reservation and folding),
  claims inside a pipeline (report()), `_RunShortener.fit` and `measured`,
  per-expression `_fitted` tokens, `_compile_tokens`, `_check_literal`
  (now `_literal`, run per dictionary), the blocked-first sort key and
  `_Dictionary.blocked`/`added`/`bundled`. core.py 1325 -> 1223
  statements, 540 -> 480 branches; check() 111 -> 17 statements
  (`_detect` 82).
- Codex pass 16 items: [P1] German `s+c+h+` retried inside every "s" run
  (`"s" * 16000` 1.28s, x15.5): now `(?<!s)s+c+h+` (same matches); audited
  every normalization pattern (Spanish `\bll(?=vowel)` and `r{2,}`, French
  and German translation tables, whitespace collapse, invisible-character
  stripping, run shortening) and every per-check `_RunIndex` pattern: all
  succeed or fail in one linear scan. Scaling tests for 26 runs of
  normalization trigger characters across the 4 languages. [P1] `_holds`
  rebuilt an index per block occurrence ("shit " * 32000 with only "shit"
  bundled and blocked: 12.9s, x13.8): deleted; 0.81s, x4.1, test added.
  [P2] "as$*hell" with block=["$"] and [P2] "12-34-5678" with both entries
  blocked: fixed by the restructure, tests added.
- Compound parts are also compared in their normalized spelling (German
  "beschaemendes" is read as "beshaemendes"; 142 German, 23 Spanish and 2
  French parts gain a spelling): needed for the block pipeline's context
  ("ipfe" before "beschaemendes"), and it fixes the bundled pipeline too:
  German sweep 232 -> 226 failing texts, nothing newly failing, corpora
  unchanged.
- Intended behaviour change: a block word glued to a bundled word no longer
  makes the bundled word a compound ("classocciso" with block=["class"]
  masks only "class"; the bundled word is judged as without the block
  list). On 4 x 200 generated block cases (r16 vs now, 2 severity levels,
  10 texts each, $S/blockcmp.py) 14-19% mask less than r16; in an English
  sample 347 of 350 are this case, and 3 are block words starting inside a
  doubled letter they share with a glued bundled word ("clitorus|shiti"):
  such a boundary reading needs a selected neighbour, which is now in the
  other pipeline, and accepting context words instead would bring back
  "git|tite". Never less than without the block list (strict probe: 0
  violations in 72,000 checks).
- Test changes: caching (the bundled dictionary is shared whatever the
  block list; `blocks` is cached by its own options), folded "$$$"/"$$"
  occurrences (now: every occurrence masked, reports never overlap),
  the pattern-driver false-positive patch moves to `blocks`, the
  shared-character test reports in text order ("!a" keeps its span).
- Differential vs r16, all 40 corpus runs: 0 differing results (with the
  restructure alone, before the compound spelling, also 0); sweep German
  -6, others identical; dictionary scan identical (845). Windowed/
  compacted vs full rescan: 0 diffs with and without block entries.
  Throughput and tiny checks within 1% with and without block lists;
  building a filter with a new block list 22ms -> 4-7ms (the bundled
  dictionary is reused); adversarial worst cases equal or faster
  ("* " * 200 all languages 0.94s -> 0.88s), distinct-hex block micro-test
  0.42s -> 0.47s (x4.2).

## 2026-10-07 (fix/codex-review-hardening, Codex review pass 15)
- P2 block entries below minimum_severity suppressed reportable matches
  ("wanker" with block=["wanker"] at high: clean, "****er" without it): its
  exact hit was required and excluded the overlapping "wank", then was not
  reported. Entries below minimum_severity now have no hits at all, and
  hits stopped being selection candidates: they are masked whatever the
  pass selects (no `required`, no `splits_a_hit`, no `_Candidate.span`); a
  reported reading strictly holding one is reported instead (`_holds`), one
  overlapping it partly besides it, a reading with the same span adds
  nothing. The first pass weighs a reading by what it covers outside the
  hits first, then by all it covers (`_Candidate.spare`), so a reading
  reaching past a hit ("ge!" in "passage!") or across the gap between two
  ("kkk, kkk") still wins as without the entry.
- Monotonicity (adding block=[B] never unmasks a character), found by a
  probe ($S/blockmono.py: B = an entry, its prefix/suffix/inner part/
  extension or an ordinary word; 12 texts; every minimum_severity; 4
  languages) and fixed:
  * Words only the block list adds (`_Dictionary.added`) are selected after
    the bundled words' readings, among themselves, next to those and
    weighed by what those leave; they are reported but not masked in the
    working text, so the bundled words' later passes run as without them
    ("zugerauch" took "zugeraucht"'s letters before its second pass).
  * Readings sharing an original character that normalization split in two
    ("ssch" read as "sh" between two "schmaehliches"; "ä" as "ae"): the
    later report used to be dropped; each now reports what is left of it.
    `test_matches_never_share_original_characters` changes accordingly
    ("!ä#": "zz-e" is reported as "#").
  Strict invariant over all characters, final code: 0 violations in
  216,000 checks (3 seeds x 4 languages x 300 cases x 5 levels x 12
  texts), and 432,000 on the step before; r15 fails 5 of the 8 regression
  texts. Property test in the suite (55 cases, ~15s).
- P2 separator-obfuscated block entries with long runs: block-only words'
  expressions are built from the word as the language normalizes it
  (`normalize`, so Spanish "rrr" -> "r" and German "sch" -> "sh" as in the
  text) with every run longer than shortening leaves cut to the shortest
  run it leaves for those characters, its last letter reading on
  (`_RunShortener.fit`, `retained()` replaces `kept()`; `_compile_tokens`).
  Chosen over matching block expressions on a separate unshortened text,
  which would need a second normalization, finder and run bounds for words
  that may be hundreds of characters long: this keeps the same expression
  machinery, so worst-case time is unchanged. Trade-off: such a word's
  obfuscated form matches any run of that letter at least as long as
  shortening keeps it, which the shortened text cannot tell apart anyway.
  Bundled words are never refitted (shortening keeps their runs).
  Expressions of replaced words drop their id()-keyed stop classes, so a
  reused id never finds a stale one. Property test: random letters with an
  interior run of 1..30, each separator, 4 languages (r15 fails all four);
  probe over 7,440 texts: 0 failures.
- Simplifications: `_CandidateFinder` no longer merges changed spans (they
  arrive merged, `_merge_spans`); `_select` lost `required`.
- Differential vs r15, all 40 corpus runs: 0 differing results; sweep (789)
  and dictionary scan (845) identical. Windowed/compacted vs full rescan: 0
  diffs, also with 17 block entries. Throughput and tiny checks within 1%,
  filters with block lists too; adversarial worst cases +1-7% ("*-" * 150
  English 0.31s -> 0.34s, "* " * 200 all languages 0.89s -> 0.94s).
  core.py 1251 -> 1325 statements.

## 2026-10-07 (fix/codex-review-hardening, Codex review pass 14)
- P1 quadratic with a block-only dictionary (d32a146): since pass 11 the run
  shortener ignored block entries entirely, so with only block entries no
  run was shortened, and the leading-run split loop matched every suffix of
  an unbounded first-letter run (block=["x-z"], "x" * 8000 + "-z": 0.88s,
  x14.7 per x4). Two fixes:
  * Split work is bounded by itself: splits are tried only within the last
    max(_GIVE_BACK_LIMIT, runs.kept(character)) characters of the run (a
    reading before it whose last letter takes the run reads all of it and
    gives back at most _GIVE_BACK_LIMIT; shortening keeps no longer runs),
    and each split matches only the slice from the split. With shortening
    switched off (test double) "x" * 8000 + "-z" is 3ms, x0.1.
    `_RunShortener.kept()` reports the per-character retained length.
  * Block entries feed the shortener's letter classes again; only their run
    lengths and literal periods do not count (`measured` = bundled words).
    This also restores block-only matching of long runs, lost since pass 11:
    block=["xy"] masked nothing in "x" * 80 + "y" (r11 masked it; a first
    letter shared with the next one runs at most 64 characters unshortened).
- Audit of loops bounded by the shortener (every loop on the candidate path,
  for unshortened runs): leading-run splits (fixed above), trailing-run scans
  in `_readings` and `_end_before_profanity` (capped at _GIVE_BACK_LIMIT + 1
  characters; the latter was an unbounded character loop), give-back (3
  positions, slices cut to `reach`), follows after a cut (longest_word starts,
  first hit), word-gap retry (one per gap, gaps bounded by the entry's
  tokens), cross-word and joined-word guards (O(match) once per reading,
  windows bounded by the longest entry), `evaluate` (O(match): elision,
  hex with per-token verdicts, Scunthorpe contexts cut to the window, at
  most 3 `_chain_needs` calls, false positives length-checked),
  `splits_a_hit` (only the hits a reading overlaps), block hits (token
  roots cached), `_select` (linear), finditer first-letter runs (start
  guard). Scaling tests: block-only letter, letter at both ends, separator,
  emoji, digit and mixed-case runs (r14: letter x14.5, the others linear as
  their tokens are literals), and split readings with shortening disabled.
- P2 runs mixing a letter with its substitutes ("bitchΗhell" with Greek
  capital eta, "shittτtwat" with tau): runs were found by comparing
  lowercased characters. They are now runs of the characters the first
  letter's class accepts (`_ExpressionInfo.first_run`) or the last letter's
  (`last_letter`), wildcards excluded, in `_readings` and in the give-back.
  Runs ending in the substitute ("bitchΗell") also needed the end-side
  mirror: readings ending inside a deliberate final run (3+ characters, or
  not two equal plain letters) with a right-neighbour requirement
  (`_Reading.after`); two equal plain letters stay the word's own double, so
  two split halves of "gittite" still cannot support each other.
- Junction checks vs r14: plain runs 1..10 (4 x 20,000 texts) 0 broken,
  German 835 more fixed, the rest unchanged; English junctions whose shared
  run mixes the letter with a substitute ($S/junction3.py, 13,500 texts):
  10,678 fixed, 0 broken, 1 left ("assess$kankfuck": "assess" is a word).
  Tests: substitute-mixed runs (Greek eta/tau/kappa, "5", "$", "š", "3",
  "é") at every length up to the retained run + 3, three run shapes each;
  clean counterparts.
- Differential vs r14, all 40 corpus runs: 0 lost, 1 gained (French
  "prcsns" read as porc|cons, a consonant chain), 12 masks grown (native
  "asssht" as ass|shat, "asssckr" as ass|suck, "bummmst"; the rest
  cross-language fragments inside "**"). Sweep identical (789), dictionary
  scan identical (845), windowed/compacted vs full rescan 0 diffs.
  Throughput and tiny checks within 1%; adversarial worst cases +2-10%
  ("*-" * 150 English 0.29s -> 0.32s), scaling ratios ~x4.
  core.py 1227 -> 1251 statements.

## 2026-10-07 (fix/codex-review-hardening, Codex review pass 13)
- P2 split limit (8da405c): `_readings` tried leading-run splits only at the
  first 3 letters (`_GIVE_BACK_LIMIT`), while the run shortener keeps up to
  7 letters of a run in English (4 German/Spanish, 3 French; 8 for the hex
  digits a-f in every language), so "bitch" + "hhh" + "hell" gave
  "*********ell" and "shitttttwat" "********wat". The split loop now walks the
  whole leading run of the match as the working text holds it, which the
  shortener caps per character, so the work per match stays bounded; the
  adjacent-match requirement (`_Reading.before`) is unchanged.
- Junction check ($S/junction2.py: 2,000 random pairs of bundled entries
  A+B with A's last letter = B's first per language, junction run 1..10, so
  20,000 texts per language) vs r13: fixed English 10,332, German 774,
  French 4,078, Spanish 6,096 (all at run lengths >= 4, a few at 3); broken
  0. Still partial at runs >= 2, the same at every length and in r13 too:
  German 172 (normalization reads stretched "s" + "sch" as "sh": "un-
  serioes|sschändlich"), Spanish 175 ("rr" normalized to "r":
  "descojonar|retrasada"), French 1 ("especee|speces"). Run length 1 (the
  letter shared, so both words cannot be whole) is reported but not
  expected to mask fully.
- Tests: every run length 2 .. retained + 3 for one pair per language (and
  three English pairs; r13 fails 6 of the 7), and clean counterparts with
  long runs inside ordinary words.
- Differential vs r13, all 40 corpus runs: 0 lost, 0 gained, 0 mask or match
  changes. Sweep identical (789), dictionary scan identical (845).
  Windowed/compacted vs full rescan: 0 diffs. Timing vs r13: throughput,
  tiny checks, adversarial worst cases and scaling ratios within 1%;
  split-heavy shapes ("bitch" + "h" * 8 + "hell", "shit" + "t" * 8 + "wat",
  "a" * 8 + "ss", repeated 500x) 5-15% slower, still x3-4 per x4.

## 2026-10-07 (fix/codex-review-hardening, Codex review pass 12)
- P1 quadratic candidate generation (6a72a6f): follows of glued copies led
  back to matches other copies had already reached, and each was re-judged
  ("twat" * 320: 51,360 reading evaluations vs 3,240 at 80 copies; 640 copies
  1.43s, x13 per x4; "shit-ass", "dickwad", "twaat" likewise). Each raw match
  is now judged once per expression and pass (`seen` in check()); a raw span
  fully determines its readings and follows, so this changes no result.
  "twat" * 640: 0.13s, x3.7. Regression test over 7 shapes (plain,
  stretched, stretched end, long, mixed entries, hyphenated entry,
  separator; r12 x10.9-14.7, now x3.7-4.0).
- P2 boundary readings: "bitttchhell" gave "********ell" (r11 masked all):
  the leading-run trim only split runs of three or more, so "hell" [7,11)
  was never a reading after "bitttch". The trim now splits any run of the
  first letter (up to _GIVE_BACK_LIMIT), and a trimmed reading (`_Reading.
  before`) only counts next to a chosen reading ending where it starts (it
  gets the left need in every alternative), so a double that is the word's
  own invents no compound ("git|tite", "wop|pish"). Trailing splits at both
  sides were tried and dropped: two split readings of one double supported
  each other ("git|t|tite").
- Junction sweep ($S/junction.py: 6,000 random pairs of bundled English
  entries A+B with A's last letter = B's first, the junction letter doubled
  and tripled, 11,998 texts): r12 11,540 fully masked, now 11,991; 451
  fixed, 0 broken; the 7 left need a run longer than the give-back limit
  ("peeenusssss...").
- Differential vs r12, all 40 corpus runs: 0 lost, 0 gained, 1 mask grown
  ("cockknobbing"), 4 match changes ("fuck|knob" instead of "fuckk|nob").
  Sweep: identical (789). Dictionary scan: 845, identical set and masks.
  Windowed/compacted vs full rescan: 0 diffs (65,903 texts x 3 x 4).
